WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.640
Speaker 1: Republicans have seen the polling
and they are terrified that running strongly

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.830
against all abortions is going to crush them
in November.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:15.370
So we have to understand now on the left,
the Republicans have have seen the polling.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.949
They know the numbers in the aftermath of
the repeal of Roe v Wade by the Supreme Court,

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:22.869
the overturning of Roe v Wade by the Supreme
Court.

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:27.279
We have seen a dramatic shift in polling for
these November midterm elections.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:30.160
And I've told you that we're going to look
at the numbers tomorrow or Thursday.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.760
But the odds that Democrats keep the Senate
are now roughly 2 to 1.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.770
Democrats still expected to lose the House,
but by a smaller margin than at any point

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.960
during this midterm election cycle.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:50.710
And much of it much of it is because of the
anger about Roe v Wade being overturned and

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.739
the fundamental right to an abortion in most
cases disappearing.

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:58.440
Republicans are aware of this, and we are
now seeing a number of different Republicans,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:04.290
either in interviews or on their websites,
change or certainly restructure the way that

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:09.940
they talk about abortion, some of them removing
altogether their anti-choice stances from

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.540
their respective websites.

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:17.550
So last week, I brought you the story of this
Democrat in Michigan who won in a swing district

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:22.340
in a special election by focusing in on abortion
just straight up one.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.270
And it's abundantly clear that this is a big
risk for Republicans.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:30.920
We now have a clip from this is from yesterday
or maybe the day before yet?

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.500
No, this is from yesterday.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.500
This is John.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.410
I believe it's pronounced Odie D might be
day.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:41.250
This is the Republican Senate candidate in
Colorado.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:48.510
He went on Fox News and he made it very clear
he supports women's right to choose an abortion

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:54.320
early in the pregnancy and even later when
there are particular circumstances.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.780
Take a listen to this, a very different sounding
Republican.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.590
Speaker 2: Martha, thanks for having me on
today.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:01.590
I really appreciate.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:02.590
Speaker 3: It.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:03.590
You know, your race was very interesting.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.590
One to watch.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:08.550
You beat a candidate who was more of a Trump
supporter.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.750
You are sort of running in a more moderate
lane.

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:17.230
And yet your opponent, the current Senator,
Michael Bennet, is is looking to sort of peg

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.220
you that way a little bit more.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:21.860
Here's an example in an ad that dropped on
Friday.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:22.860
Watch us.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:23.860
Speaker 1: Dropped.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.850
Speaker 3: It makes the race for Senate even
more important.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.030
Michael Bennet has always fought to protect
a woman's right to make her own health care

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:32.030
decisions.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.460
But Joe O'Dea opposes the law protecting abortion
access in Colorado.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.230
So we want to give you a chance to respond
to that, because clearly the Roe v Wade decision

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.550
is becoming a factor in races all across the
country.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.360
We're going to see a lot of ads like that.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.850
Are they accurately portraying your view on
this, sir?

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.860
Speaker 2: Oh, that that ads dishonest.

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.480
It's at best.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.930
I came out early on in this election and said
that I supported a woman's rights early on

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:02.630
in the pregnancy and even later on, you know,
with mitigating circumstances.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:08.030
So I've been very vocal about that issue,
ran my primary on that, and we ended up winning.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:16.210
Speaker 1: You know, so this is very interesting
because I can't tell you that if Joe O'Dea

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:22.020
makes it, makes it to the Senate that he would
actually be a pro-choice vote.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.810
I can't say what all we all we can look at
is what he's saying today.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:31.310
And he has made, I believe, the correct calculation
that at this point when it came.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:36.060
To Democrats and even moderate Republicans.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:41.730
It is not a good idea to take these wacky
anti-choice views in a state like Colorado

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:48.040
when you are running in the Senate now, if
you are running in a particular red district

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.390
for the House in Alabama, it's a very different
story.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:56.450
But he's correctly sort of taking the pulse
of the electorate in Colorado and the pulse

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:57.760
of the country.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:03.000
And if he is going to defeat Bennet and get
to the Senate with that electorate and the

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.240
entire state of Colorado as the voting constituency,
he can't be a wacky, anti-choice or now he

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.000
may be an anti-choice or personally, but he
has made the correct calculation that he can't

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.599
really be running that way.

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:16.080
This is not super unique.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:19.919
We're seeing instances of this elsewhere as
well in Arizona.

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:28.560
Four days ago, Blake Masters right wing nut
backtracks on abortion and scrubs his campaign

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:29.560
website.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.560
This is all from the last week.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:36.910
So Blake Masters realizing I might want to
ease up on some of the anti-abortion stuff.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.500
Another example here.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:45.020
Republican congressional candidate and state
Senator Tom Barrett totally removed the values

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:51.840
section of his campaign website, which touted
his anti-abortion position and history with

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.280
the pro-life movement.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.130
Also making the calculation that, listen,
even if these are my views and even if this

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.940
is how I'm ultimately going to vote, if the
people elect me, I don't necessarily want

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.590
to be promoting this and making it front and
center.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.340
This is a very important shift.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:09.940
Now, what are what are the going to be the
substantive repercussions of such a shift?

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:16.620
We actually don't know yet because part of
it is, are these Republicans actually becoming

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.910
mildly pro-choice or are they just lying because
they see that that's what they need to do

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:23.280
in order to get themselves elected?

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.530
And it won't actually change anything if and
when they do.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:29.139
That's a it's a predictive question.

00:05:29.139 --> 00:05:30.330
We're just guessing.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.620
We don't have the answer.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.900
But the fact that they're feeling the need
to change their messaging on this issue is

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.900
already extraordinarily important.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:39.780
Let me know what you make of this.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:45.210
Are these candidates is there going to be
a wave of pro-choice Republicans or are these

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.710
merely the lies they're willing to tell in
order to get themselves elected?

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.620
Because they've sort of put their finger to
the wind and feel the way that it's blowing.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:52.889
Let me know.

